  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fog development likely tonight with mostly SKC,\ndecoupled winds, and RHs already around 100%. Fog may become\ndense at times and a dense fog advisory may be needed in spots. \nREFS and HREF data suggest A 50-70% chance for VIS<1mi across \nmost of ENC outside of the far Nern tier of counties and OBX. \nAs a result, have a mix of patchy and areas of fog in the grids\nacross much of ENC in the early morning hours. \n\nTrough axis aloft will continue to push further off the coast \nwith heights building in its wake as ridge expands from the \nGulf. In the lower half, high pressure meanders off the coast \nallowing SEerly winds to veer further to a S-SWerly direction \nthrough the overnight. Upper ridging will be over the Mid- \nAtlantic into the end of the week before pushing offshore with \nthe approach of an upper level trough in the Ohio River Valley \nthat will push east as well. Pocket of dry air will be\nentrenched over SECONUS leading to quiet weather and a \ncontinued warming trend into the end of the week. By the end of \nthe week the area could flirt with record highs given the set up\n(SEE CLIMATE SECTION FOR MORE DETAILS). \n\nFor reference, normal highs in early October are around 80 degrees, \nand forecast highs are in the mid to upper 80s for much of ENC, with \nsome 90s possible inland today, likely low 90s on FRI. \nPersistent southerly flow and increasing low- level moisture \nwill lead to muggier lows as well, well above Normal, mid to\nupper 60s FRI and SAT mornings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...No real changes in forecast thinking this weekend \nand into next week as precipitation chances return as Gulf \nmoisture opens up and a cold front drapes across the Sern \nAppalachians and pushes SEward slowly on SAT. This moisture\ntransport opening up has slowed down slightly with the SECONUS \nridge holding on a little bit longer into FRI night when the \nridge axis aloft finally pivots off the coast, allowing Gulf\nmoisture to reach ENC late FRI night. As the next wave aloft\napproaches, the moisture will squeeze into ENC, elevating rain \nchances for the weekend. While thunderstorms will be possible, \nthe threat for severe weather is low at this time.\n\nThe front loses steam as it approaches the coast, potentially \nbriefly stalling over Coastal Carolinas, ahead of a second \nreinforcing front spurred from the Great Lakes by a stronger trough \naloft. For now, ensemble guidance is showing about a 30-50% chance of \nexceeding 1\" of rain with this system. Any rain will be helpful, \nespecially for the coastal plain of ENC that is currently in \nmoderate to extreme drought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Precip chances do look to diminish by TUE next \nweek as the fronts push offshore and a strong high pressure \nbuilds in from the N and W. Stiff NEerly breeze (peaking MON\nnight into TUE) will lead to building seas and elevated water\nlevels. Astronomical tide levels will luckily be on the way\ndown, but seas building to 7ft in mid length periods (7-9sec)\ncould lead to further beach and dune erosion concerns across\nNOBX, Hatteras, and Ocracoke Islands due to severely diminished\ndune structures here. Winds remain out of the N-NE into late-\nweek, but speeds diminish to 10-15kt WED onward. For now, TUE \nwill probably pose the greatest risk for coastal flooding \nconcerns.",
